1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,224
[Sung Si-kyung's We Would Meet]

2
00:00:03,277 --> 00:00:08,414
So today, Mika Nakashima said she wanted to join us.

3
00:00:08,415 --> 00:00:10,415
It's my first time meeting her too.

4
00:00:10,416 --> 00:00:13,522
She said she can't drink alcohol and would like to have samgyetang.

5
00:00:13,523 --> 00:00:16,769
I was going to make samgyetang, but I ended up making dak gomtang instead, with all the meat deboned.

6
00:00:16,770 --> 00:00:19,938
We've got dak muchim and dak gomtang! It's a proper meal today.

7
00:00:19,939 --> 00:00:20,631
And with that

8
00:00:20,632 --> 00:00:22,892
I heard she's having a concert in South Korea in June.

9
00:00:22,893 --> 00:00:25,661
I will have an interview

10
00:00:25,662 --> 00:00:27,383
and ask her what she thinks about Korea.

11
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,447
[Mika Nakashima We Would Meet]

12
00:00:32,708 --> 00:00:33,907
Excuse me.

13
00:00:33,908 --> 00:00:34,861
Welcome.

14
00:00:34,862 --> 00:00:36,185
Nice to meet you.

15
00:00:36,186 --> 00:00:38,430
I'm Mika Nakashima.

16
00:00:38,431 --> 00:00:39,585
Pardon me.

17
00:00:39,586 --> 00:00:40,507
What's this?

18
00:00:40,508 --> 00:00:42,847
It's ramen and

19
00:00:43,231 --> 00:00:45,369
I heard you like alcohol, so

20
00:00:45,370 --> 00:00:48,078
this is alcohol from my hometown.

21
00:00:48,079 --> 00:00:49,215
Is your hometown Kagoshima?

22
00:00:49,216 --> 00:00:50,031
Yes, that's right.

23
00:00:50,032 --> 00:00:50,924
I did a little research.

24
00:00:50,925 --> 00:00:53,215
Thank you.

25
00:00:53,216 --> 00:00:56,431
It's whiskey. I don't drink whiskey myself, but

26
00:00:56,432 --> 00:00:58,215
I brought it because it seems to be all the rage these days.

27
00:00:58,632 --> 00:01:01,446
[Thank you for the gift]

28
00:01:01,447 --> 00:01:05,092
[Today's guest: Mika Nakashima]
I made dak gomtang instead of samgyetang.

29
00:01:05,093 --> 00:01:05,954
What's that?

30
00:01:05,955 --> 00:01:10,938
Samgyetang has a whole chicken in it.

31
00:01:10,939 --> 00:01:13,016
And it's served with glutinous rice.

32
00:01:13,017 --> 00:01:14,508
Oh, it's like a hot pot.

33
00:01:14,509 --> 00:01:18,077
It's a bit thick and has glutinous rice in it.

34
00:01:18,078 --> 00:01:21,231
Dak gomtang is a lighter version than that.

35
00:01:21,232 --> 00:01:22,601
Oh, it's mild.

36
00:01:22,602 --> 00:01:25,155
People in South Korea make and eat it like this at home.

37
00:01:25,156 --> 00:01:26,954
[Dak gomtang]
Is it really this authentic?

38
00:01:26,955 --> 00:01:28,478
Try it and see for yourself.

39
00:01:28,479 --> 00:01:30,477
- Thank you, I'll enjoy it.
- Please, dig in.

40
00:01:30,478 --> 00:01:36,185
I got some delicious kimchi from a friend's store.

41
00:01:36,186 --> 00:01:37,504
It might be a bit spicy.

42
00:01:37,505 --> 00:01:38,370
Really?

43
00:01:38,371 --> 00:01:39,414
Are you okay with spicy food?

44
00:01:39,415 --> 00:01:42,894
I don't like it too spicy, but I do like kimchi.

45
00:01:46,294 --> 00:01:47,878
Wow, it's delicious!

46
00:01:48,786 --> 00:01:51,374
Oh, there's rice too?

47
00:01:51,417 --> 00:01:54,417
I cooked it separately because if you put it together, the soup gets cloudy.

48
00:01:55,017 --> 00:01:56,631
Rather than getting cloudy

49
00:01:56,632 --> 00:01:58,232
it becomes thicker.

50
00:02:00,479 --> 00:02:02,786
[This is really tasty...]

51
00:02:03,248 --> 00:02:04,662
Is this made from chicken too?

52
00:02:04,663 --> 00:02:06,356
Yes, it's from the same chicken.

53
00:02:06,357 --> 00:02:11,633
I added more chicken to make the dak gomtang broth richer.

54
00:02:11,634 --> 00:02:14,786
[Try a bite of dak muchim too!]

55
00:02:16,094 --> 00:02:17,433
[It's delicious!]

56
00:02:17,434 --> 00:02:19,802
[You've got great cooking skills]

57
00:02:20,217 --> 00:02:22,386
I wonder if the kimchi is spicy. I'll give it a try.

58
00:02:22,387 --> 00:02:24,371
It might be a bit spicy.

59
00:02:32,725 --> 00:02:34,064
[Huh?]

60
00:02:34,065 --> 00:02:35,387
[It's not spicy...]

61
00:02:36,633 --> 00:02:39,264
[Gulp]

62
00:02:39,265 --> 00:02:41,433
[The spiciness hits you later]

63
00:02:41,434 --> 00:02:44,678
But it's delicious.

64
00:02:44,679 --> 00:02:48,878
[Can you speak Korean?]

65
00:02:48,879 --> 00:02:52,433
I know the common phrases.

66
00:02:52,434 --> 00:02:56,617
I can understand what's being said, but I can't respond fluently.

67
00:02:56,618 --> 00:02:59,617
Mika Nakashima is really famous in South Korea.

68
00:02:59,618 --> 00:03:01,033
Thank you.

69
00:03:01,034 --> 00:03:02,695
What's interesting is

70
00:03:02,696 --> 00:03:04,664
maybe it's because of Park Hyo-shin.

71
00:03:04,665 --> 00:03:05,803
That's right.

72
00:03:05,804 --> 00:03:09,618
The cover of "Snow Flower" was such a huge hit

73
00:03:09,619 --> 00:03:12,249
and also, people who like JPOP

74
00:03:12,250 --> 00:03:14,526
have liked you for a long time.

75
00:03:14,664 --> 00:03:20,387
[A leading figure in 2000s J-POP]

76
00:03:20,388 --> 00:03:26,126
[Famous as the original singer of "Snow Flower"!]

77
00:03:26,249 --> 00:03:32,034
[Actively working between South Korea and Japan!]

78
00:03:32,035 --> 00:03:35,694
Even people who aren't fans know Mika Nakashima.

79
00:03:35,695 --> 00:03:37,587
You have name recognition in South Korea.

80
00:03:37,588 --> 00:03:38,925
Thank you.

81
00:03:38,926 --> 00:03:42,202
I recently participated in the "Hamada Song Festival."

82
00:03:42,203 --> 00:03:43,633
The "Hamada Song Festival"?

83
00:03:43,634 --> 00:03:45,833
It's a show in Japan.

84
00:03:45,834 --> 00:03:49,371
Mr. Hamada is the MC and everyone sings together.

85
00:03:49,372 --> 00:03:52,880
There, you memorize lyrics for group songs

86
00:03:52,881 --> 00:03:55,618
without knowing which part you'll sing

87
00:03:55,619 --> 00:03:59,388
and when your turn comes, you have to sing it all from memory.

88
00:03:59,389 --> 00:04:00,896
You have to memorize it?

89
00:04:00,897 --> 00:04:03,387
That's the plan, but they give you about three minutes.

90
00:04:03,388 --> 00:04:05,819
They played "Snow Flower" there.

91
00:04:05,820 --> 00:04:09,788
I remember working really hard to memorize the lyrics.

92
00:04:09,789 --> 00:04:16,033
[The lyrics of "Snow Flower" that I worked hard to memorize]

93
00:04:16,034 --> 00:04:18,419
[Lyrics I still remember]

94
00:04:18,420 --> 00:04:19,803
That must have been tough.

95
00:04:19,804 --> 00:04:22,696
But it's truly a beautiful song.

96
00:04:22,697 --> 00:04:25,342
Not to brag, but

97
00:04:25,343 --> 00:04:27,050
I also watched the drama "I'm Sorry, I Love You."

98
00:04:27,051 --> 00:04:29,096
(My song was covered) in that drama.

99
00:04:29,097 --> 00:04:33,049
It was moving to have it used back then.

100
00:04:33,050 --> 00:04:34,788
Have you ever met Park Hyo-shin?

101
00:04:34,789 --> 00:04:36,726
I haven't met him yet.

102
00:04:36,727 --> 00:04:39,157
I don't know if that day will ever come.

103
00:04:39,158 --> 00:04:42,295
He's not the type to appear in public often...

104
00:04:42,296 --> 00:04:44,435
Well, maybe someday if there's a chance.

105
00:04:44,436 --> 00:04:46,065
Do you think he'll watch this broadcast?

106
00:04:46,066 --> 00:04:50,050
[Hoping for a Mika X Hyo-shin meeting]
Hyo-shin, you should meet Nakashima-san.

107
00:04:50,051 --> 00:04:51,327
Someday!

108
00:04:51,328 --> 00:04:53,734
How many times have you been to South Korea?

109
00:04:54,296 --> 00:04:55,772
Including work...

110
00:04:55,773 --> 00:04:57,957
I've also come frequently for personal reasons.

111
00:04:57,958 --> 00:04:59,049
I've lost count now.

112
00:04:59,050 --> 00:05:03,773
[Also appeared on "Jae Friends"!]

113
00:05:03,774 --> 00:05:07,454
Is this your first concert in South Korea?

114
00:05:07,455 --> 00:05:11,589
[Scheduled for June 13 at Ilsan KINTEX Hall as part of the "Asian Tour 2026"!]

115
00:05:11,590 --> 00:05:14,573
Have you ever performed at such a large venue in South Korea?

116
00:05:14,574 --> 00:05:16,020
No, I haven't.

117
00:05:16,021 --> 00:05:17,004
That's quite a challenge.

118
00:05:17,005 --> 00:05:20,020
What if no one shows up?

119
00:05:20,234 --> 00:05:22,541
Don't you always get anxious?

120
00:05:22,542 --> 00:05:23,896
Yeah, of course.

121
00:05:23,897 --> 00:05:25,373
Right? When the curtain goes up

122
00:05:25,374 --> 00:05:29,357
I always end up having nightmares before a performance...

123
00:05:29,634 --> 00:05:31,213
Like the curtain rises and there's no one there.

124
00:05:31,214 --> 00:05:33,710
Or I go on stage without having memorized everything.

125
00:05:33,711 --> 00:05:35,756
What? Really?

126
00:05:35,757 --> 00:05:37,834
I only have those kinds of dreams.

127
00:05:37,835 --> 00:05:39,311
I had a third try at the college entrance exam.

128
00:05:39,312 --> 00:05:43,434
[The recurring nightmare I had during my third try at the college entrance exam was]

129
00:05:43,435 --> 00:05:45,465
I hadn't studied at all yet

130
00:05:45,466 --> 00:05:47,895
and I'm taking an exam that my life depends on.

131
00:05:47,896 --> 00:05:50,572
Sitting at a desk

132
00:05:50,573 --> 00:05:53,465
I have no idea! My life is over...

133
00:05:53,466 --> 00:05:56,326
Even when I try to cheat, I'm being watched like a hawk by the proctor.

134
00:05:56,327 --> 00:05:58,126
I wake up thinking, "What do I do? Aaaah!"

135
00:05:58,127 --> 00:05:59,588
Is it really that bad though...

136
00:05:59,589 --> 00:06:01,663
- Isn't it kind of cute, in a way?
- It's kind of like that.

137
00:06:01,664 --> 00:06:04,880
Like a singer performing at a concert with no audience.

138
00:06:04,881 --> 00:06:08,643
I often wake up scared from dreams like that.

139
00:06:09,710 --> 00:06:11,725
I mentioned it earlier, didn't I?

140
00:06:11,726 --> 00:06:14,786
Mika Nakashima is known not just among JPOP fans but also among South Koreans.

141
00:06:14,787 --> 00:06:18,494
Everyone's like, "Oh, I know Mika Nakashima."

142
00:06:18,495 --> 00:06:19,925
Thank you.

143
00:06:19,926 --> 00:06:22,017
It would've been nice if you were at "Would Sing," too.

144
00:06:22,018 --> 00:06:24,232
Do you know any of my songs?

145
00:06:24,233 --> 00:06:26,649
I'm sorry, I'm not really familiar.

146
00:06:27,187 --> 00:06:28,956
I'm sorry about that...

147
00:06:28,957 --> 00:06:30,433
On the flip side, I'm happy.

148
00:06:30,434 --> 00:06:31,972
Really? Why?

149
00:06:31,973 --> 00:06:34,002
I feel a bit awkward saying this myself, but

150
00:06:34,003 --> 00:06:35,602
I'm so well-known in South Korea.

151
00:06:35,603 --> 00:06:36,464
Really?

152
00:06:36,465 --> 00:06:38,479
When someone says, "Who are you?" it makes me kind of happy.

153
00:06:38,480 --> 00:06:41,924
It's not like you're completely unknown.

154
00:06:41,925 --> 00:06:45,216
I'm also working hard in Japan, but

155
00:06:45,217 --> 00:06:47,694
I want to become a newcomer again.

156
00:06:47,695 --> 00:06:49,986
It's really exciting to take on new challenges again.

157
00:06:49,987 --> 00:06:53,571
It's more exciting than knowing everything already.

158
00:06:53,572 --> 00:06:56,325
Meeting and talking to someone for the first time

159
00:06:56,326 --> 00:06:59,109
there's a certain joy in that, isn't there?

160
00:06:59,110 --> 00:07:01,063
So don't worry about me.

161
00:07:01,064 --> 00:07:04,509
If you don't know something, just say so.

162
00:07:04,510 --> 00:07:07,201
[Honesty is the best policy]

163
00:07:07,202 --> 00:07:10,032
What do you usually like?

164
00:07:10,033 --> 00:07:13,308
If it's food I like, just white rice.

165
00:07:13,309 --> 00:07:17,073
- White rice is delicious, like rice balls.
- That's right.

166
00:07:17,074 --> 00:07:19,908
I'm the type who's happy with just that.

167
00:07:19,909 --> 00:07:22,000
But, you know, for health reasons

168
00:07:22,001 --> 00:07:25,232
I do try to eat a balanced diet, though...

169
00:07:25,233 --> 00:07:28,778
But I read in an article before that

170
00:07:28,779 --> 00:07:32,090
white rice gets a bit of a bad rap.

171
00:07:32,091 --> 00:07:34,124
- That's right.
- They say it makes you gain weight.

172
00:07:34,125 --> 00:07:37,324
People say brown rice is better, but that's not always the case.

173
00:07:37,325 --> 00:07:39,740
When I'm really hungry, I can eat about two bowls of rice.

174
00:07:40,093 --> 00:07:41,524
I eat a lot, you see.

175
00:07:41,525 --> 00:07:42,709
How much is two bowl of rice?

176
00:07:42,710 --> 00:07:45,616
[*1go = about a large instant rice pack (300g)]
I said 2go... Oh, right, you don't use "go" here.

177
00:07:45,617 --> 00:07:47,570
Maybe it's not that much.

178
00:07:47,571 --> 00:07:49,985
I don't eat it all at once. I spread it out over the day.

179
00:07:49,986 --> 00:07:51,078
Ah, right.

180
00:07:51,079 --> 00:07:53,016
- I was really surprised.
- I don't eat it all at once, though.

181
00:07:53,017 --> 00:07:55,862
I thought you were a competitive eater.

182
00:07:55,863 --> 00:08:00,216
Still, I do eat about 2go a day by myself.

183
00:08:00,217 --> 00:08:02,001
What about the side dishes?

184
00:08:02,002 --> 00:08:05,692
Honestly, I don't need much in the way of side dishes.

185
00:08:05,693 --> 00:08:07,830
As long as I have rice, I'm good.

186
00:08:07,831 --> 00:08:12,399
[Now I'm trying to eat more side dishes too]

187
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:13,553
Why's that?

188
00:08:13,554 --> 00:08:16,200
White rice does have some nutrients, but

189
00:08:16,201 --> 00:08:18,569
still, it's not enough on its own.

190
00:08:18,570 --> 00:08:23,123
I love it so much I could live on just that.

191
00:08:23,124 --> 00:08:26,399
[Trying to eat a balanced diet for health reasons]

192
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:29,045
What's your impression of South Korea?

193
00:08:29,046 --> 00:08:33,153
At first, I would come with friends sometimes...

194
00:08:33,154 --> 00:08:35,231
I came more for travel than work.

195
00:08:35,232 --> 00:08:36,999
For fun?

196
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,353
Everyone says South Korea's food is delicious.

197
00:08:39,354 --> 00:08:41,414
I really just went around eating.

198
00:08:41,415 --> 00:08:44,260
Well, I am South Korean, after all.

199
00:08:44,261 --> 00:08:46,922
When you go to Japan, the food there is delicious too.

200
00:08:46,923 --> 00:08:48,246
I wonder if it's the same feeling.

201
00:08:48,247 --> 00:08:49,076
That's right.

202
00:08:49,077 --> 00:08:50,400
In my opinion

203
00:08:50,401 --> 00:08:54,937
I think it's because of the unique atmosphere and seasoning that you can't find in Japan.

204
00:08:54,938 --> 00:08:56,568
South Korean cuisine.

205
00:08:56,569 --> 00:08:59,045
And since there's no Japanese style in South Korea, too.

206
00:08:59,046 --> 00:09:02,615
Is it because the cooking methods are different that it's so intriguing?

207
00:09:02,616 --> 00:09:06,384
There's a show that introduces that with Yutaka Matsushige.

208
00:09:06,385 --> 00:09:08,661
Like "K-foodie meets J-foodie" that airs on Netflix.

209
00:09:08,662 --> 00:09:10,507
Oh?

210
00:09:10,508 --> 00:09:11,307
That's been going on for a while.

211
00:09:11,400 --> 00:09:14,660
[Is that person this person?]

212
00:09:14,661 --> 00:09:16,988
Ain't I the worst? I am watching that show.

213
00:09:17,338 --> 00:09:20,348
[(Trying to remember...)]

214
00:09:20,368 --> 00:09:22,661
Should I call this Season 2?

215
00:09:22,908 --> 00:09:25,676
With a model named Ayaka Miyoshi.

216
00:09:25,677 --> 00:09:27,507
A new season has started.

217
00:09:27,508 --> 00:09:28,708
I watched that and thought

218
00:09:28,709 --> 00:09:31,214
that naengmyeon place looks good.

219
00:09:31,215 --> 00:09:33,168
You should go there. It's delicious.

220
00:09:33,169 --> 00:09:35,230
When I went last time

221
00:09:35,231 --> 00:09:36,722
oh, when I came to South Korea

222
00:09:36,723 --> 00:09:39,522
I searched for that naengmyeon restaurant

223
00:09:39,523 --> 00:09:43,090
I went a few times, but it was closed, so I couldn't eat there.

224
00:09:43,091 --> 00:09:43,953
Let's do it this way.

225
00:09:43,954 --> 00:09:47,091
"I want to eat this, and I am around here."

226
00:09:47,092 --> 00:09:49,121
If you ask, "Where do you think is good?"

227
00:09:49,122 --> 00:09:51,168
I'll send you the information.

228
00:09:51,169 --> 00:09:52,905
Is that okay?

229
00:09:52,906 --> 00:09:54,768
[Then I'll leave it to you]

230
00:09:54,769 --> 00:09:57,799
It's the most frustrating when Japanese people come to South Korea and

231
00:09:57,800 --> 00:10:02,044
eat at places I can't accept.

232
00:10:02,045 --> 00:10:04,552
If they go back saying, "South Korea is the best," it makes me so sad.

233
00:10:04,553 --> 00:10:07,213
[(Totally get it)]
"There's a tastier place," or something like that.

234
00:10:07,214 --> 00:10:10,337
- I see!
- Don't just go to tourist spots like Myeong-dong.

235
00:10:10,338 --> 00:10:13,136
Myeong-dong is nice, but if you go a bit further

236
00:10:13,137 --> 00:10:14,891
there's a deeper South Korea waiting for you.

237
00:10:14,892 --> 00:10:15,706
I see.

238
00:10:15,707 --> 00:10:18,090
It's a lack of information.

239
00:10:18,091 --> 00:10:21,044
I just want people to eat the most delicious things possible.

240
00:10:21,045 --> 00:10:23,367
Please, I really ask you.

241
00:10:23,368 --> 00:10:25,367
Today, I'm eating right now, but...

242
00:10:25,767 --> 00:10:26,998
If you were to go out at night, where's a good place?

243
00:10:26,999 --> 00:10:28,644
What do you feel like eating?

244
00:10:28,645 --> 00:10:30,397
What would be good?

245
00:10:30,398 --> 00:10:32,859
Earlier for lunch, I had water parsley.

246
00:10:32,860 --> 00:10:35,181
- Water parsley?
- Something like a water parsley hot pot.

247
00:10:35,182 --> 00:10:39,998
What kinds of water parsley hot pot are there?

248
00:10:40,106 --> 00:10:42,744
Isn't water parsley more of a supporting role, right?

249
00:10:42,745 --> 00:10:45,161
Adding water parsley to something...

250
00:10:45,162 --> 00:10:45,813
I had spicy fish roe stew.

251
00:10:45,814 --> 00:10:46,672
Spicy fish roe stew.

252
00:10:46,673 --> 00:10:49,167
[Had spicy fish roe stew for lunch]

253
00:10:49,168 --> 00:10:52,165
Do you usually eat that?

254
00:10:52,166 --> 00:10:54,782
That sounds more like a side dish or snack, especially in South Korea...

255
00:10:54,783 --> 00:10:56,766
I had it with rice, though.

256
00:10:56,767 --> 00:10:59,733
Which neighborhood is your accommodation in?

257
00:10:59,734 --> 00:11:01,235
I forgot the name.

258
00:11:01,236 --> 00:11:02,523
It's Goyang!

259
00:11:02,524 --> 00:11:03,313
That's far away.

260
00:11:03,314 --> 00:11:08,374
Yeah, it was quite a distance to get here.

261
00:11:08,593 --> 00:11:10,792
I guess I need to look into it a bit more.

262
00:11:10,793 --> 00:11:12,708
That's not really my usual stomping ground.

263
00:11:12,709 --> 00:11:15,042
You seem to know this area well.

264
00:11:15,043 --> 00:11:16,476
Goyang isn't Seoul, you know.

265
00:11:16,477 --> 00:11:18,234
Right, that's what I said.

266
00:11:18,235 --> 00:11:21,266
[Saitama: A prefecture located north of Tokyo, part of the metropolitan area]
It kind of feels like Saitama.

267
00:11:21,267 --> 00:11:24,326
But hey, if you look around, you'll probably find it.

268
00:11:24,327 --> 00:11:25,768
If it's Goyang...

269
00:11:25,769 --> 00:11:30,331
A bit further, there's a place famous for Korean-style eel.

270
00:11:30,332 --> 00:11:32,154
Korean-style eel?

271
00:11:32,155 --> 00:11:33,632
You're curious, right?

272
00:11:33,633 --> 00:11:34,515
Do you like eel?

273
00:11:34,516 --> 00:11:37,432
Not really a fan.

274
00:11:37,433 --> 00:11:39,238
Those tiny bones really bother me.

275
00:11:39,660 --> 00:11:41,235
Eel bones?

276
00:11:41,236 --> 00:11:44,375
[Eel bones?]

277
00:11:44,376 --> 00:11:46,091
They clean it thoroughly, you know.

278
00:11:46,092 --> 00:11:47,720
They do clean the bones, but

279
00:11:47,721 --> 00:11:49,861
usually in Japan, you see.

280
00:11:49,862 --> 00:11:52,899
Eel always has those tiny bones, you know.

281
00:11:52,900 --> 00:11:55,917
I'll look into it a bit more.

282
00:11:55,918 --> 00:11:57,745
Thank you.

283
00:11:57,746 --> 00:11:59,572
Or if you could just decide on the menu, that'd be great.

284
00:11:59,573 --> 00:12:00,532
Got it.

285
00:12:00,533 --> 00:12:05,775
But I really like shrimp, crab, or clams.

286
00:12:05,776 --> 00:12:07,581
I really love them.

287
00:12:07,582 --> 00:12:08,980
So you like them, not that you can't eat them, right?

288
00:12:08,981 --> 00:12:09,936
I love them!

289
00:12:13,484 --> 00:12:15,743
[A city located at the southern end of Kyushu]
Are you from Kagoshima?

290
00:12:16,573 --> 00:12:20,302
My father is around 70 or 80 years old.

291
00:12:20,303 --> 00:12:24,137
We traveled around Kyushu for his birthday.

292
00:12:24,138 --> 00:12:25,504
For your father's birthday?

293
00:12:25,505 --> 00:12:27,567
Yes, the whole family, all ten of us.

294
00:12:27,568 --> 00:12:31,925
We rented a bus with help from a friend in Japan.

295
00:12:31,926 --> 00:12:32,906
We went on a hot springs tour.

296
00:12:32,907 --> 00:12:34,247
[Oh, I see]

297
00:12:34,248 --> 00:12:36,623
Actually, there are hot springs in Kagoshima.

298
00:12:36,624 --> 00:12:38,201
Though they're not very well-known.

299
00:12:38,202 --> 00:12:41,960
I lived in a very rural area of Kagoshima.

300
00:12:41,961 --> 00:12:47,316
Nearby is a place called Yunomoto... In Chinese characters, it means "hot water."

301
00:12:47,317 --> 00:12:49,333
And "place," emphasizing its hot springs.

302
00:12:49,334 --> 00:12:51,989
There were hot springs nearby.

303
00:12:51,990 --> 00:12:54,044
Ever since I was a kid...

304
00:12:54,045 --> 00:12:56,377
For example, if my skin got rough

305
00:12:56,378 --> 00:12:59,471
I'd think, "Going to the hot springs will fix it," and I'd go.

306
00:12:59,472 --> 00:13:00,361
Like going to a hospital.

307
00:13:00,362 --> 00:13:02,356
That's right.

308
00:13:02,357 --> 00:13:03,930
At what age did you move to the city?

309
00:13:03,931 --> 00:13:06,155
I was 18.

310
00:13:06,156 --> 00:13:07,579
Back then, I was dreaming of becoming a singer.

311
00:13:07,580 --> 00:13:10,274
I moved to the city after deciding to become a singer.

312
00:13:10,275 --> 00:13:13,087
So, does that mean someone chose you?

313
00:13:13,088 --> 00:13:14,386
That's right.

314
00:13:14,387 --> 00:13:16,711
I guess you could call it a demo tape that I made.

315
00:13:16,712 --> 00:13:18,230
Oh, it was that kind of era.

316
00:13:18,231 --> 00:13:21,647
- Yes, that's right.
- I made a demo tape and went around to record companies.

317
00:13:21,648 --> 00:13:24,171
Yes, and the interesting thing is

318
00:13:24,172 --> 00:13:28,183
a friend knew someone who was a songwriter and composer.

319
00:13:28,184 --> 00:13:31,991
I was close with them, so we often went to karaoke together.

320
00:13:31,992 --> 00:13:35,301
They asked me to sing a guide vocal.

321
00:13:35,302 --> 00:13:37,841
It seems they sent out that demo tape.

322
00:13:37,842 --> 00:13:40,095
Probably hoping to get a song written for them.

323
00:13:40,096 --> 00:13:44,256
Not to promote, but to sell the song.

324
00:13:44,257 --> 00:13:47,506
I was doubtful, like, "Could I really become a singer?"

325
00:13:47,507 --> 00:13:48,669
You mean as a guide vocalist?

326
00:13:48,670 --> 00:13:50,815
Yes, I never expected it.

327
00:13:50,816 --> 00:13:54,446
I'm not bragging, saying I never thought about it.

328
00:13:54,447 --> 00:13:59,656
It felt like a miraculous stroke of luck.

329
00:13:59,657 --> 00:14:01,192
Were you excited or nervous?

330
00:14:01,193 --> 00:14:03,978
I was really nervous, honestly.

331
00:14:03,979 --> 00:14:05,806
Do you usually get nervous on stage?

332
00:14:05,807 --> 00:14:06,948
Yes, very much so.

333
00:14:06,949 --> 00:14:07,726
Even during concerts?

334
00:14:07,727 --> 00:14:08,830
Yes, it's really bad.

335
00:14:08,831 --> 00:14:12,264
When it comes to broadcasting, I'm nervous to the point of death.

336
00:14:12,265 --> 00:14:13,649
This broadcast right now?

337
00:14:13,650 --> 00:14:14,092
No, I mean music broadcasts?

338
00:14:14,093 --> 00:14:15,617
Oh my gosh

339
00:14:15,618 --> 00:14:17,245
I understand about music broadcasts.

340
00:14:17,246 --> 00:14:18,426
Like on terrestrial TV.

341
00:14:18,427 --> 00:14:19,026
I understand.

342
00:14:19,027 --> 00:14:21,823
When it's not your own sound, but a mix of many people.

343
00:14:21,824 --> 00:14:23,119
During a live broadcast.

344
00:14:23,120 --> 00:14:25,333
If you make a mistake, it's game over.

345
00:14:25,334 --> 00:14:28,326
The whole nation is watching, and among them

346
00:14:28,327 --> 00:14:30,938
I think there are quite a few people who don't like me.

347
00:14:30,939 --> 00:14:32,784
It makes me want to scream, "Aaaah."

348
00:14:32,785 --> 00:14:34,065
But when it comes to concerts

349
00:14:34,066 --> 00:14:36,448
I don't get nervous at all.

350
00:14:36,449 --> 00:14:38,376
The fact that they paid to come see me.

351
00:14:38,377 --> 00:14:40,787
That's definitely true.

352
00:14:40,788 --> 00:14:43,484
I'm only thinking about how to satisfy them.

353
00:14:43,485 --> 00:14:45,353
Do you get nervous during concerts too?

354
00:14:45,354 --> 00:14:47,781
Concerts too, and

355
00:14:47,782 --> 00:14:53,116
just like you said, TV is more nerve-wracking.

356
00:14:53,117 --> 00:14:56,293
I'm so scared I can't stand it.

357
00:14:56,294 --> 00:14:58,510
I kind of get that feeling.

358
00:14:58,511 --> 00:15:00,265
Your voice has such a unique color when you sing.

359
00:15:00,266 --> 00:15:04,091
You're so charming, and your breathing technique is beautiful.

360
00:15:04,092 --> 00:15:05,746
- Thank you.
- But I also

361
00:15:05,747 --> 00:15:09,569
had the chance to sing a song by Mika Nakashima.

362
00:15:09,570 --> 00:15:12,305
When I watch old broadcasts or materials

363
00:15:12,306 --> 00:15:14,099
I think, "Oh, this person is nervous."

364
00:15:14,100 --> 00:15:16,717
I guess it's because I'm a singer that I know.

365
00:15:16,718 --> 00:15:18,542
That's how it is.

366
00:15:18,543 --> 00:15:21,713
I've never been satisfied with a recording, or a live broadcast.

367
00:15:21,714 --> 00:15:23,297
I understand.

368
00:15:23,298 --> 00:15:26,485
I've never felt like, "Ah, I sang 100% perfectly."

369
00:15:26,486 --> 00:15:27,105
That's true, really.

370
00:15:27,106 --> 00:15:29,251
Always hitting about 80%!

371
00:15:29,252 --> 00:15:33,264
"Is this good enough?"

372
00:15:33,265 --> 00:15:35,516
That's about all I can do, really.

373
00:15:35,517 --> 00:15:37,771
But that's just my personality, I guess.

374
00:15:37,772 --> 00:15:39,354
Hmm, maybe so.

375
00:15:39,355 --> 00:15:41,857
Aren't there quite a few people who don't really care about it?

376
00:15:41,858 --> 00:15:42,694
There are!

377
00:15:42,695 --> 00:15:45,663
They say "Enjoy it!" but what's there to enjoy?

378
00:15:45,664 --> 00:15:48,517
[(Totally relatable)]
It's natural to feel nervous.

379
00:15:48,518 --> 00:15:51,721
What's it like on Korean music broadcasts?

380
00:15:51,722 --> 00:15:53,425
It's nerve-wracking.

381
00:15:53,426 --> 00:15:54,503
I really get nervous.

382
00:15:54,504 --> 00:15:55,646
Are the monitors okay?

383
00:15:55,647 --> 00:15:59,064
Honestly, I'm not used to in-ear monitors.

384
00:15:59,065 --> 00:16:00,092
Didn't you debut during the era when in-ear monitors became a thing?

385
00:16:00,092 --> 00:16:03,396
Didn't you debut during the era when in-ear monitors became a thing?

386
00:16:03,397 --> 00:16:06,626
I debuted in 2000, so

387
00:16:06,627 --> 00:16:09,128
it is pretty close. I debuted in 2001.

388
00:16:09,129 --> 00:16:11,437
[stage monitor]
I've been using stage monitors for so long

389
00:16:11,438 --> 00:16:15,438
I'm still not quite used to these in-ear monitors that go in your ears.

390
00:16:15,439 --> 00:16:17,769
I just can't seem to get the hang of them.

391
00:16:17,770 --> 00:16:18,585
I end up not knowing what's what.

392
00:16:18,586 --> 00:16:19,958
Exactly, they're just too close.

393
00:16:19,959 --> 00:16:24,522
With stage monitors, you can feel it in your body.

394
00:16:24,523 --> 00:16:29,362
Lately, I've realized I need to train with in-ear monitors.

395
00:16:29,363 --> 00:16:32,416
Older Korean musicians aren't used to in-ear monitors.

396
00:16:33,057 --> 00:16:36,534
They kept using wedge monitors, which is fascinating.

397
00:16:36,535 --> 00:16:42,213
Plus, 20 or 30 years ago, the sound systems were a mess.

398
00:16:42,214 --> 00:16:44,608
You couldn't hear a thing.

399
00:16:44,609 --> 00:16:46,750
How did they sing without hearing their own voice?

400
00:16:46,751 --> 00:16:49,462
Could that be considered a safety measure?

401
00:16:49,463 --> 00:16:50,808
A safety measure?

402
00:16:50,809 --> 00:16:52,712
Because there were no in-ear monitors.

403
00:16:52,713 --> 00:16:57,124
They'd press one ear to hear their voice internally.

404
00:16:57,125 --> 00:16:59,247
They had no choice but to hear it that way.

405
00:16:59,248 --> 00:17:01,994
I wonder how they managed to sing in such poor conditions.

406
00:17:01,995 --> 00:17:03,823
Those older musicians are just so talented.

407
00:17:03,824 --> 00:17:05,054
Absolutely.

408
00:17:05,055 --> 00:17:07,707
Just like we talked about, using their body to hear themselves.

409
00:17:07,708 --> 00:17:08,438
They could sing anywhere.

410
00:17:08,439 --> 00:17:12,183
They trained themselves to sing anywhere, without relying on technology.

411
00:17:12,639 --> 00:17:13,505
That's true, but

412
00:17:13,506 --> 00:17:17,255
wouldn't it be okay to just use one in-ear monitor?

413
00:17:17,256 --> 00:17:20,004
A lot of people do use just one in-ear monitor, for sure.

414
00:17:20,005 --> 00:17:21,224
That way, you can still hear the music.

415
00:17:21,225 --> 00:17:22,287
Exactly.

416
00:17:22,288 --> 00:17:24,685
But when I wear both, I start feeling uneasy.

417
00:17:24,686 --> 00:17:26,453
[(Totally relatable)]
You can't tell if the sound is right or not.

418
00:17:26,454 --> 00:17:27,923
Yeah, it makes it impossible to know for sure.

419
00:17:27,924 --> 00:17:29,411
But without in-ear monitors

420
00:17:29,936 --> 00:17:31,519
I can't do it.

421
00:17:32,274 --> 00:17:35,346
Unless the monitors are really good

422
00:17:35,347 --> 00:17:37,881
or if it's just a piano or guitar

423
00:17:37,882 --> 00:17:39,858
in those cases, it's fine.

424
00:17:39,859 --> 00:17:42,958
But doing all ballads like that is too much.

425
00:17:43,775 --> 00:17:47,649
Many people do perform with just one in-ear monitor.

426
00:17:47,650 --> 00:17:51,005
Everyone's concerned about hearing health, so they wear both.

427
00:17:51,006 --> 00:17:54,727
They say it's best to get used to wearing both.

428
00:17:54,728 --> 00:17:55,887
But

429
00:17:55,888 --> 00:17:57,542
I end up taking them out anyway.

430
00:17:57,543 --> 00:18:00,029
I see.

431
00:18:00,030 --> 00:18:03,678
Do they cover Korean songs at Korean concerts?

432
00:18:03,679 --> 00:18:04,515
No, they don't.

433
00:18:04,516 --> 00:18:05,292
Is it too much?

434
00:18:05,293 --> 00:18:07,490
But honestly, I want to do it.

435
00:18:07,491 --> 00:18:08,680
What would be good?

436
00:18:08,681 --> 00:18:10,252
[Hmm]

437
00:18:10,253 --> 00:18:11,755
Give me a moment to think.

438
00:18:11,756 --> 00:18:15,446
It's sudden, but could you tell me what it is?

439
00:18:15,447 --> 00:18:19,400
Don't you have any favorite songs?

440
00:18:19,401 --> 00:18:23,807
It's probably originally from South Korea, but I'm not sure.

441
00:18:23,808 --> 00:18:25,895
From "My Sassy Girl."

442
00:18:25,896 --> 00:18:26,985
"I Believe"?

443
00:18:26,986 --> 00:18:29,742
That song, actually

444
00:18:29,743 --> 00:18:30,987
was supposed to be my song to sing.

445
00:18:30,988 --> 00:18:32,365
What?

446
00:18:32,366 --> 00:18:33,474
It was taken away from me.

447
00:18:33,475 --> 00:18:34,265
Really?

448
00:18:34,266 --> 00:18:35,117
To Shin Seung-hun.

449
00:18:35,118 --> 00:18:37,014
Please sing it.

450
00:18:37,015 --> 00:18:38,693
I've covered it before in South Korea.

451
00:18:38,694 --> 00:18:39,597
I really love that song.

452
00:18:39,598 --> 00:18:41,646
There's a version of "I Believe" by Sung Si-kyung.

453
00:18:41,647 --> 00:18:48,138
[Sung 20 years ago, "I Believe"]

454
00:18:48,139 --> 00:18:52,665
There's a legendary producer in South Korea named Kim Hyung-suk

455
00:18:52,666 --> 00:18:55,327
and I happened to be with his agency.

456
00:18:55,328 --> 00:18:58,261
He had me sing various songs

457
00:18:58,262 --> 00:19:01,027
but he felt sorry this time, so

458
00:19:01,028 --> 00:19:02,818
he decided not to ask for a favor again.

459
00:19:02,819 --> 00:19:04,216
[(Regretful)]

460
00:19:04,217 --> 00:19:06,242
So Shin Seung-hun ended up doing it.

461
00:19:06,243 --> 00:19:07,495
Yes, it was Shin Seung-hun.

462
00:19:07,496 --> 00:19:10,883
But I do believe there's a connection between songs and their performers.

463
00:19:10,884 --> 00:19:14,847
But it's a song that every Korean loves.

464
00:19:15,063 --> 00:19:17,972
- Really?
- I'm really glad Shin Seung-hun did it.

465
00:19:17,973 --> 00:19:21,369
I tried covering it, but it just didn't have the same feel.

466
00:19:21,370 --> 00:19:24,987
A group from Japan

467
00:19:24,988 --> 00:19:26,842
covered it.

468
00:19:26,843 --> 00:19:27,591
Oh? Who?

469
00:19:27,592 --> 00:19:33,321
["Full Of Harmony" did a Japanese version cover]

470
00:19:33,322 --> 00:19:35,876
(If you're going to perform it at a concert,) I think it should be in Korean.

471
00:19:35,877 --> 00:19:37,933
Since it's a Korean concert, after all.

472
00:19:37,934 --> 00:19:38,878
I wonder if I can memorize it.

473
00:19:38,879 --> 00:19:40,149
They'll be happy, for sure.

474
00:19:40,150 --> 00:19:41,438
So, can you help me with it?

475
00:19:41,991 --> 00:19:43,741
Yes, of course.

476
00:19:43,742 --> 00:19:46,873
I guess I have no choice.

477
00:19:46,874 --> 00:19:48,300
What will you do for me in return?

478
00:19:48,301 --> 00:19:50,337
What should I do?

479
00:19:51,998 --> 00:19:54,579
Please continue to love South Korea.

480
00:19:54,580 --> 00:19:56,682
That's a given.

481
00:19:56,683 --> 00:20:00,681
[What song would be suitable?]
What Korean song would suit Mika Nakashima?

482
00:20:00,682 --> 00:20:03,275
Shall we exchange songs with each other?

483
00:20:03,276 --> 00:20:04,859
Let's pick a song that suits.

484
00:20:04,860 --> 00:20:06,503
My song?

485
00:20:06,504 --> 00:20:09,295
Probably one of the most popular songs.

486
00:20:09,296 --> 00:20:10,956
There's a song called "On the Street."

487
00:20:10,957 --> 00:20:13,671
(The composer) Yoon Jong-shin actually had a specific intention when creating this song.

488
00:20:13,672 --> 00:20:16,035
He made it thinking he wanted to give it to Mika Nakashima.

489
00:20:16,036 --> 00:20:17,100
Oh? I think I've heard it before.

490
00:20:17,101 --> 00:20:19,681
Send it to me later.

491
00:20:19,682 --> 00:20:22,832
Originally, "On the Street" was written with Mika Nakashima in mind.

492
00:20:22,833 --> 00:20:23,926
I ended up singing it.

493
00:20:23,927 --> 00:20:25,878
Then, from my songs, as I please

494
00:20:25,879 --> 00:20:28,266
can I send a few songs that I think would suit?

495
00:20:28,930 --> 00:20:29,755
Sure.

496
00:20:29,756 --> 00:20:31,557
Since it's a ballad, it matches the image.

497
00:20:31,558 --> 00:20:34,774
I'm sure you can sing anything, but

498
00:20:34,775 --> 00:20:37,610
the popular image is definitely ballads.

499
00:20:37,611 --> 00:20:38,983
Will it have a similar feel, I wonder?

500
00:20:38,984 --> 00:20:40,062
Yes.

501
00:20:40,063 --> 00:20:42,791
The Korean male version of Nakashima.

502
00:20:42,792 --> 00:20:48,581
Right, a slightly dark tone suits you too.

503
00:20:48,582 --> 00:20:53,300
[What song would be good?]
What options are there?

504
00:20:53,301 --> 00:20:55,461
But the pronunciation is difficult, as expected.

505
00:20:55,462 --> 00:20:56,812
The performance is in June.

506
00:20:56,813 --> 00:20:58,741
Already? Hold on a second.

507
00:20:58,742 --> 00:21:02,289
Can I memorize the lyrics?

508
00:21:02,290 --> 00:21:06,257
No, you don't have to sing it perfectly.

509
00:21:06,258 --> 00:21:09,862
I tried my best, so please be a little lenient.

510
00:21:09,863 --> 00:21:10,892
I'm sure Koreans will love it.

511
00:21:10,893 --> 00:21:15,911
[Hoping the Korean fans will like it]

512
00:21:16,613 --> 00:21:19,095
How many songs are we performing at the concert?

513
00:21:19,096 --> 00:21:20,112
About 20 songs, I guess?

514
00:21:20,112 --> 00:21:23,142
About 20 songs?

515
00:21:23,143 --> 00:21:24,190
Do you dance too?

516
00:21:24,192 --> 00:21:25,420
No, we don't dance.

517
00:21:25,421 --> 00:21:29,650
We move around a bit, but there's no choreography.

518
00:21:29,866 --> 00:21:31,256
Do you dance?

519
00:21:31,281 --> 00:21:32,758
Yes, for the audience.

520
00:21:32,759 --> 00:21:33,542
Really?

521
00:21:33,543 --> 00:21:36,603
[If it's to make people laugh...]

522
00:21:36,604 --> 00:21:40,742
Do you have any plans for activities in Japan?

523
00:21:40,743 --> 00:21:42,557
There are plans, but

524
00:21:42,558 --> 00:21:43,573
can't you talk about it yet?

525
00:21:43,574 --> 00:21:44,680
Yeah, there's something...

526
00:21:44,681 --> 00:21:46,665
Japan is really strict, right?

527
00:21:46,666 --> 00:21:48,035
It's strict.

528
00:21:48,036 --> 00:21:52,111
Honestly, we don't even know how much will be decided.

529
00:21:52,112 --> 00:21:56,095
But there are various plans in the works.

530
00:21:56,096 --> 00:21:58,203
The concert in South Korea was a huge success, so

531
00:21:58,204 --> 00:22:01,742
I hope it makes them think, "Let's do it next year too."

532
00:22:01,743 --> 00:22:03,927
I'd love to do it every year if possible.

533
00:22:03,928 --> 00:22:07,265
It's the best, isn't it?

534
00:22:07,266 --> 00:22:09,142
I'll come to watch.

535
00:22:09,143 --> 00:22:10,834
Please sing a song.

536
00:22:10,835 --> 00:22:12,803
- What?
- Then please sing a song.

537
00:22:12,804 --> 00:22:15,173
Take your time to think about it.

538
00:22:15,174 --> 00:22:17,157
Doesn't Japan have a guest culture?

539
00:22:17,158 --> 00:22:18,172
Yes, it does.

540
00:22:18,173 --> 00:22:18,988
Oh? It does, huh.

541
00:22:18,989 --> 00:22:21,756
When doing a live concert in Japan

542
00:22:21,757 --> 00:22:23,588
we invite friends

543
00:22:23,589 --> 00:22:27,571
or they come to celebrate anniversaries and such.

544
00:22:27,572 --> 00:22:29,557
I haven't done it much myself, though.

545
00:22:29,558 --> 00:22:30,956
It's quite common.

546
00:22:30,957 --> 00:22:33,356
In South Korea, it varies by person

547
00:22:33,357 --> 00:22:36,296
but over 90% are divided into Part 1 and Part 2

548
00:22:36,297 --> 00:22:39,403
and a guest comes as a surprise guest appearance in the middle.

549
00:22:39,404 --> 00:22:44,433
They sing about two songs while the main artist changes outfits.

550
00:22:44,434 --> 00:22:48,510
And if they come, I go next time.

551
00:22:48,511 --> 00:22:51,833
It's done without payment, just among colleagues.

552
00:22:51,834 --> 00:22:54,141
There's that kind of culture.

553
00:22:54,142 --> 00:22:55,848
But in Japan...

554
00:22:55,849 --> 00:22:57,878
It seems like there's nothing like that.

555
00:22:57,879 --> 00:23:01,032
Artists perform solo

556
00:23:01,033 --> 00:23:03,848
and it's over in 1 hour and 40 minutes!

557
00:23:03,849 --> 00:23:05,572
But in South Korea, it's a bit longer.

558
00:23:05,573 --> 00:23:06,648
I see.

559
00:23:06,649 --> 00:23:08,233
I perform for about three and a half hours.

560
00:23:08,234 --> 00:23:09,295
That's long!

561
00:23:09,296 --> 00:23:10,155
[Three and a half hours?]

562
00:23:10,156 --> 00:23:12,432
About 24 or 25 songs.

563
00:23:12,433 --> 00:23:15,063
I'm having a concert in early May.

564
00:23:15,064 --> 00:23:15,970
Early May?

565
00:23:15,971 --> 00:23:17,802
The 2nd, 3rd, and 5th!

566
00:23:17,803 --> 00:23:19,186
[It's outdoors, so come and join us]

567
00:23:19,187 --> 00:23:21,833
Outdoors? That sounds fun.

568
00:23:24,141 --> 00:23:27,294
If I think of anything, I'll suggest it.

569
00:23:27,295 --> 00:23:28,710
Feel free to decline without any pressure.

570
00:23:28,711 --> 00:23:32,986
Sure, I understand.

571
00:23:32,987 --> 00:23:35,924
I've always dreamed of that.

572
00:23:35,925 --> 00:23:39,708
Anywhere is fine, whether it's Korea, Japan, or China.

573
00:23:39,709 --> 00:23:41,955
If there's a song you'd like to sing together

574
00:23:41,956 --> 00:23:44,201
it can't be forced.

575
00:23:44,202 --> 00:23:48,371
I look forward to working well together in the future.

576
00:23:48,372 --> 00:23:49,971
Well, that's all from me.

577
00:23:49,972 --> 00:23:51,355
That was Ms. Nakashima.

578
00:23:51,709 --> 00:23:53,863
Would you like to say a few words to your Korean fans?

579
00:23:53,864 --> 00:23:55,340
What should I say?

580
00:23:55,341 --> 00:23:59,339
Thank you to all the fans who always support me.

581
00:23:59,340 --> 00:24:02,170
I'll work hard for this live concert too.

582
00:24:02,171 --> 00:24:04,201
I hope you'll come.

583
00:24:05,924 --> 00:24:06,847
Please dance for us.

584
00:24:06,848 --> 00:24:08,031
No, I don't want to.

585
00:24:09,955 --> 00:24:11,724
Your Korean fans would love it.

586
00:24:11,725 --> 00:24:15,109
But this time, it's a bit different from the usual live concert.

587
00:24:15,110 --> 00:24:17,262
It's different.

588
00:24:17,263 --> 00:24:21,139
I usually have ballet dancers.

589
00:24:21,140 --> 00:24:24,339
I always bring them, but this time there won't be any dancers.

590
00:24:24,340 --> 00:24:28,936
It's a mix of an acoustic feel and my usual style.

591
00:24:28,937 --> 00:24:32,707
Is that format also used in Japan?

592
00:24:32,708 --> 00:24:35,384
I do both, acoustic and the usual style.

593
00:24:35,385 --> 00:24:38,476
No, is this concert specifically planned for South Korea.

594
00:24:38,477 --> 00:24:43,323
Or is the completed stage going to be performed anywhere?

595
00:24:43,324 --> 00:24:46,184
This tour follows that format.

596
00:24:46,185 --> 00:24:48,585
So you're bringing that here, huh?

597
00:24:49,062 --> 00:24:51,753
I'm looking forward to it.

598
00:24:51,754 --> 00:24:53,231
Thank you for coming today.

599
00:24:53,232 --> 00:24:55,307
- No, thank you.
- I had a great time, thank you.

600
00:24:55,308 --> 00:24:59,691
That was Mika Nakashima.

601
00:24:59,708 --> 00:25:02,338
[Bonus clip]

602
00:25:02,339 --> 00:25:05,138
[Recommending songs to sing at the Korean concert...]
I think a song by Lee So-ra would be perfect.

603
00:25:05,139 --> 00:25:07,261
What song by Lee So-ra should we recommend?

604
00:25:07,262 --> 00:25:09,862
For example

605
00:25:12,508 --> 00:25:16,261
the lyrics aren't too many, and the words...

606
00:25:16,262 --> 00:25:22,461
[Si-kyung's pick is Lee So-ra's "Don't Leave My Side"]

607
00:25:22,462 --> 00:25:24,707
Please send it to me.

608
00:25:24,708 --> 00:25:27,154
No, I feel like I'm about to cry already.

609
00:25:27,155 --> 00:25:28,260
It's decided!

610
00:25:28,261 --> 00:25:29,077
Please, I'm asking for your help.

611
00:25:29,078 --> 00:25:30,507
It's been decided with this.

612
00:25:30,508 --> 00:25:32,215
Come to the performance to hear this song.

613
00:25:32,216 --> 00:25:34,275
Hold on a second!

614
00:25:34,276 --> 00:25:36,692
I'll do it if I can.

615
00:25:38,707 --> 00:25:45,969
[Sung Si-kyung's "We Would Meet"]

